  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>Insurance survey work in the UK spans risk surveys, loss adjusting, property risk and fire risk, each with its own report shape</li><li>The strongest report is captured on site, not written up from memory afterwards</li><li>General inspection apps and dictation tools work but add an install, a login and per-seat cost</li><li>A WhatsApp workflow needs nothing installed because the surveyor already uses the app daily</li><li>Software produces a clean, defensible record; it does not make the survey itself valid</li></ul></div><h2>What insurance survey software is actually for</h2><p>Insurance survey is a broad label covering several distinct disciplines. A commercial insurance risk survey informs underwriting by recording construction, occupancy, protection and exposure, often summarised as COPE. A loss adjuster's site report follows a claim and establishes scope, cause and reinstatement cost. A property risk assessment examines perils such as fire, flood, escape of water and security. A fire risk assessment is a regulated activity under the Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005, frequently carried out to the PAS 79 methodology.</p><p>The software is not what makes any of these valid. The competence of the surveyor is, together with the relevant professional standard. What software does is make the report easier to complete correctly on site, harder to leave incomplete, and faster to deliver to the underwriter or claims handler. The report completed at the site beats the one reconstructed from notes two days later.</p><h2>The three types of insurance survey software</h2><h3>1. General inspection and audit apps</h3><p>Tools like iAuditor (SafetyCulture) are built for any inspection, not for insurance surveyors specifically. You can build a survey template in them, but templates need setup and the app needs training. Pricing is usually per seat, which is expensive for claims and underwriting teams where office staff read reports but never file one.</p><h3>2. Dictation and document tools</h3><p>Many surveyors dictate into a Word template and tidy it later. It is fast to start but the tidying happens off site, the structure lives in the surveyor's head, and the office has no live view of outstanding work. It suits a sole practitioner and scales poorly across a team.</p><h3>3. Conversation-based workflows</h3><p>The newest category drops the app. The surveyor receives the survey as a WhatsApp conversation, replies with text, voice note or photo, and the report generates as a PDF and web record. There is nothing to install because every surveyor already uses WhatsApp. Quickler runs here, landing the survey in the surveyor's existing chat and surfacing status on an office dashboard.</p><h2>What to look for in insurance survey software</h2><ul><li><strong>On-site completion:</strong> the tool should make it easier to complete the report during the visit, not afterwards</li><li><strong>Consistent field set:</strong> every surveyor should produce the same shaped report, so nothing gets skipped</li><li><strong>Photo and voice capture:</strong> evidence should attach to the finding, at the finding</li><li><strong>No per-seat penalty:</strong> claims handlers and underwriters who read reports should cost nothing to add. Quickler charges per report with unlimited users</li><li><strong>Defensible record:</strong> timestamp, surveyor, address and every observation, ready for a claims or underwriting file</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler produces clean PDF and web reports but is not a replica of a specific insurer's proprietary survey form, and it does not replace professional judgement or regulated methodology such as PAS 79 for fire risk or RICS standards for surveying. This is general reporting software, not legal or professional advice. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if a specific format requirement affects your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is insurance survey software?</h3><p>Insurance survey software records and reports a survey of an insured risk, whether a commercial risk survey for underwriting, a loss adjuster's site report after a claim, or a property or fire risk assessment. It captures findings, photos and recommendations, then produces a report. Quickler does this through a WhatsApp conversation with no separate app to install.</p><h3>Do surveyors need to install an app to use Quickler?</h3><p>No. Quickler runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The surveyor receives each question in their existing WhatsApp chat and replies with text, a voice note or a photo. Quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Does the software make the survey valid?</h3><p>No. The competence of the surveyor makes the survey valid, along with the relevant professional standard such as RICS survey standards or PAS 79 for fire risk. Software makes the report easier to complete on site and faster to deliver. It produces a clean record, not the judgement inside it.</p><h3>How is Quickler priced for a survey firm?</h3><p>Quickler is priced per report, not per seat. Bundles run from Quickler 50 at 50 pounds a month for 50 reports to Quickler 500 at 500 pounds a month for 500 reports, with unlimited users on every bundle. Office staff who read reports but never file one cost nothing to add.</p><h3>Which survey types does Quickler support?</h3><p>Quickler is a general workflow engine supporting commercial insurance risk surveys, loss adjuster site reports, property risk assessments and fire risk assessment surveys, among others. Each is built as its own workflow with the right field set.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/insurance-risk-survey-report-uk.html\">Commercial insurance risk survey reports</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/loss-adjuster-site-report-app-uk.html\">Loss adjuster site report app</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/property-risk-assessment-insurance-uk.html\">Property risk assessment for insurers</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/fire-risk-assessment-insurance-uk.html\">Fire risk assessment survey app</a></li></ul>",
